# 7. Core Performance Indicators

MAGNIKA should maintain a consistent set of performance indicators across its stages.

| Category | Important indicators |
|---|---|
| Microstructure | Grain size, grain-size distribution, texture |
| Magnetic response | B-H curve, permeability, saturation, coercivity |
| Internal magnetic state | Grain dispersion, boundary mismatch, domain structure |
| Material losses | Hysteresis and eddy-current losses |
| Electromagnetic machine | Ld, Lq, saliency ratio, average torque |
| Torque quality | Torque ripple |
| Electrical performance | Current, voltage, power factor, efficiency |
| Thermal performance | Winding and core temperatures |
| Mechanical performance | Stress, deformation, rotor safety |
| Economics | Material cost, manufacturing cost, system cost |

The indicators must be connected to the correct physical model. For example, grain-angle dispersion is a useful lattice-model diagnostic, but it is not itself a measured motor-performance indicator.

# 8. Scientific and Engineering Validation Principles

The project should follow these rules:

1. Never interpret a numerical trend as a real material law before calibration.
2. Keep dimensionless model parameters separate from physical material properties.
3. Preserve simulation configurations, random seeds and source-code versions.
4. Validate numerical gradients and convergence independently of optimizer success flags.
5. Use multiple independent microstructures when evaluating statistical trends.
6. Separate grain-scale, material-scale and motor-scale phenomena.
7. Compare predicted material behavior with independent measurements.
8. Compare FEM predictions with prototype measurements before claiming motor performance.
9. Report uncertainty and limitations alongside every major result.
10. Do not optimize a motor using material data outside the validated range.

